      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-3}+1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{x-3}-1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{x-3}-1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-3}+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-3}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x-3}-1}{\\sqrt{x-3}-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x-3}-1}{(\\sqrt{x-3}+1)(\\sqrt{x-3}-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x-3}-1}{(\\sqrt{x-3})^{2}-{1}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x-3}-1}{x-3-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x-3}-1}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
